    # game/books.rpy:545
    old "We take turns yelling into a shallow lake, and it ripples out in ways that rewrite years. The ground is still unstable, islands having drifted back together, anchored close enough to walk across. We drift from side-by-side to a mile away in dense fog. So sometimes we toss stones and listen for the call-response of stones hitting water, and other times the silence is all."
    new "We take turns yelling into a shallow lake, and it ripples out in ways that rewrite years. The ground is still unstable, islands having drifted back together, anchored close enough to walk across. We drift from side-by-side to a mile away in dense fog. So sometimes we toss stones and listen for the call-response of stones hitting water, and other times the silence is all."

    # game/books.rpy:547
    old "Then we’re on a ledge, separated by a pane of glass, and it distorts everything. Halfway down a cliff-face, on an unreliable outcropping. Too small. The river below too strained. There’s nothing to be done but to write on the glass and play deranged charades until we can compensate for the distortion. We have most of it figured out, but the separation is still clear, and sometimes it’s too much. When it is, we just sit there. Sometimes I think the glass is one-way. "
    new "Then we’re on a ledge, separated by a pane of glass, and it distorts everything. Halfway down a cliff-face, on an unreliable outcropping. Too small. The river below too strained. There’s nothing to be done but to write on the glass and play deranged charades until we can compensate for the distortion. We have most of it figured out, but the separation is still clear, and sometimes it’s too much. When it is, we just sit there. Sometimes I think the glass is one-way. "

    # game/books.rpy:549
    old "I’m building a bridge somewhere that doesn’t need one. The pieces are all worn and some are a bit rotten, but not all of them. My dad is building the bridge from the other side, and his pieces aren’t much better, mismatched stone and brick. Many are chipped and cracked, the mortar too dry or wet. But the bridge holds, and we work toward the middle. He looks over at me now again with his usual half-smile and I smile back. It’s nice. It’s messy and unstable and an architect’s worst nightmare, but it doesn’t matter. Even if it bends and creaks, it’ll survive a storm."
    new "I’m building a bridge somewhere that doesn’t need one. The pieces are all worn and some are a bit rotten, but not all of them. My dad is building the bridge from the other side, and his pieces aren’t much better, mismatched stone and brick. Many are chipped and cracked, the mortar too dry or wet. But the bridge holds, and we work toward the middle. He looks over at me now again with his usual half-smile and I smile back. It’s nice. It’s messy and unstable and an architect’s worst nightmare, but it doesn’t matter. Even if it bends and creaks, it’ll survive a storm."

    # game/books.rpy:551
    old "I’m sitting atop some grand redwood, looking down, and there he is, below, staring back. He hates heights. “You’re not usually here,” I say."
    new "I’m sitting atop some grand redwood, looking down, and there he is, below, staring back. He hates heights. “You’re not usually here,” I say."

    # game/books.rpy:552
    old "“I’d like to be.” "
    new "“I’d like to be.” "

    # game/books.rpy:553
    old "I smile and climb down. I didn’t want to try flying this time anyway. He hums out-of-tune and I laugh, just a little."
    new "I smile and climb down. I didn’t want to try flying this time anyway. He hums out-of-tune and I laugh, just a little."

    # game/books.rpy:554
    old "Then I fall, because I’m prone to it, and I look down to see sunny pavement leading to ferry docks. Dad sighs. “I told you not to run.” And the day before and the day before and the day before. My brother is out ahead and I’m so very young. I cry because my knees are scraped and it hurts and I’m young. I’m up and walking beside my dad, and he holds my hand. Tomorrow I’ll be running again, and falling again. And the day after and the day after."
    new "Then I fall, because I’m prone to it, and I look down to see sunny pavement leading to ferry docks. Dad sighs. “I told you not to run.” And the day before and the day before and the day before. My brother is out ahead and I’m so very young. I cry because my knees are scraped and it hurts and I’m young. I’m up and walking beside my dad, and he holds my hand. Tomorrow I’ll be running again, and falling again. And the day after and the day after."

    # game/books.rpy:556
    old "I’m old enough to not need to run. We’re in an art gallery and he’s regaling an art history class. He reads every plaque next to the empty display spaces. All the information and history that bores me to death. There’s nothing to see, just walls that don’t even hold the dust echoes of stolen artwork. Glass display cases that only scream the light they reflect. "
    new "I’m old enough to not need to run. We’re in an art gallery and he’s regaling an art history class. He reads every plaque next to the empty display spaces. All the information and history that bores me to death. There’s nothing to see, just walls that don’t even hold the dust echoes of stolen artwork. Glass display cases that only scream the light they reflect. "

    # game/books.rpy:557
    old "I’m bored to death and back. But don’t say a word of it. Listening. Just like he does for me, though surely it’s just as boring."
    new "I’m bored to death and back. But don’t say a word of it. Listening. Just like he does for me, though surely it’s just as boring."

    # game/books.rpy:558
    old "There’s one sculpture. It’s a coin, with my face on one side, his on the other. It’s rare, and he knows everything about it, telling me how valuable it is. I nod along, all the while wondering who would’ve made such a thing. Is it more art than coin? Which side would it more likely land on?"
    new "There’s one sculpture. It’s a coin, with my face on one side, his on the other. It’s rare, and he knows everything about it, telling me how valuable it is. I nod along, all the while wondering who would’ve made such a thing. Is it more art than coin? Which side would it more likely land on?"

    # game/books.rpy:560
    old "We’re sitting in a car, and I’m in the driver’s seat. We’re at a red light, but it’s going to stay red forever. “You should be mad at me,” I say."
    new "We’re sitting in a car, and I’m in the driver’s seat. We’re at a red light, but it’s going to stay red forever. “You should be mad at me,” I say."

    # game/books.rpy:561
    old "“I’m not. A little annoyed, maybe, but not mad.”"
    new "“I’m not. A little annoyed, maybe, but not mad.”"

    # game/books.rpy:562
    old "I smile, but somehow him saying that makes me feel guilty. It’d be better if he were mad, I think. “Why not?”"
    new "I smile, but somehow him saying that makes me feel guilty. It’d be better if he were mad, I think. “Why not?”"

    # game/books.rpy:563
    old "“Because it’s not a big deal. It’s not worth being angry over,” he says. He turns and looks at me with his signature half-smile. The light turns green."
    new "“Because it’s not a big deal. It’s not worth being angry over,” he says. He turns and looks at me with his signature half-smile. The light turns green."

    # game/books.rpy:565
    old "We’re in one of the houses we’ve lived in before. It’s a basement crossed with a living room, with only an unfamiliar grandfather clock, hanging from the middle of the ceiling. We’re sitting in front of it, me on the floor, my dad in a chair. I see his reflection in the pendulum, and he taps his foot out-of-sync with its rhythm. I’m only watching. Listening. Sometimes his tapping lines up with the clock, but it mostly doesn’t. I start tapping my fingers along to both, indecisive. I switch between. One, the other, both. It’s uneven and chaotic. I keep at it."
    new "We’re in one of the houses we’ve lived in before. It’s a basement crossed with a living room, with only an unfamiliar grandfather clock, hanging from the middle of the ceiling. We’re sitting in front of it, me on the floor, my dad in a chair. I see his reflection in the pendulum, and he taps his foot out-of-sync with its rhythm. I’m only watching. Listening. Sometimes his tapping lines up with the clock, but it mostly doesn’t. I start tapping my fingers along to both, indecisive. I switch between. One, the other, both. It’s uneven and chaotic. I keep at it."

    # game/books.rpy:567
    old "It’s when we’re driving down the freeway, there on the shoulder. Rolled on its side and unmoving. Something tells me it’s me. And I wonder what it’s like, the last moments. Did it know that it was dying? Does it perceive pain the same, with fear and confusion? I feel like I know your memories, watching the river of lights, too fast to follow, flowing over the midnight pavement between slowing blinks."
    new "It’s when we’re driving down the freeway, there on the shoulder. Rolled on its side and unmoving. Something tells me it’s me. And I wonder what it’s like, the last moments. Did it know that it was dying? Does it perceive pain the same, with fear and confusion? I feel like I know your memories, watching the river of lights, too fast to follow, flowing over the midnight pavement between slowing blinks."

    # game/books.rpy:568
    old "Stumbling toward the long dark, sluggish and growing cold. Bloodless. Falling to lie in wait for dawn. The sensation of fading, slow, into the never waking. Will they remember me once my blood and bones have gone? Did you know you were dying? That your legs would never again hold your weight? Do you, like the crows and elephants, comprehend death and mourn? Who will mourn for us? I think I am already mourning myself."
    new "Stumbling toward the long dark, sluggish and growing cold. Bloodless. Falling to lie in wait for dawn. The sensation of fading, slow, into the never waking. Will they remember me once my blood and bones have gone? Did you know you were dying? That your legs would never again hold your weight? Do you, like the crows and elephants, comprehend death and mourn? Who will mourn for us? I think I am already mourning myself."

